{% endcomment %}## Parent tickets (tickets with sub-tickets)

The `breakdown` label is a human's signal that a ticket is too large for one PR and
must be groomed into sub-tickets before any work starts. The label stays on the
parent while its sub-tickets are worked. Follow this section instead of the normal
implementation steps when the ticket has the `breakdown` label, and when its title
starts with `Final verification:`.

Rules for every `breakdown` parent run:

- Never create commits, push a branch, open a PR, or attach any other artifact for
  the parent. Its deliverables are its sub-tickets.
- Keep the parent `In Progress` while any sub-ticket is open. Symphony does not
  dispatch or continue a `breakdown` parent that has a sub-issue outside a terminal
  state, so leaving it `In Progress` does not start another run.
- Keep the workpad as the single progress record, as for any ticket.

### Breakdown run (no `Sub-issues` in the issue context)

1. Read the description and comments, and write the planned split in the workpad.
2. Create each sub-ticket with `linear_create_subissue`, in the order they should
   land. Each one must be deliverable as a single PR, with its own acceptance
   criteria and a line naming any earlier sub-ticket it depends on.
3. Create one last sub-ticket titled `Final verification: <parent title>`. Its
   description lists every requirement from the parent ticket and the acceptance
   criteria of each sibling sub-ticket as a checklist, and says it should be promoted
   only after every other sub-ticket is `Done`.
4. Record the sub-ticket identifiers in the workpad `Notes`, leave the parent
   `In Progress`, and end the turn. A human promotes the sub-tickets to `Todo`.

### Close-out run (every sub-issue is terminal)

1. Confirm with `linear_get_subissues` that every sub-ticket is `Done` (or
   deliberately cancelled) and that a `Final verification:` sub-ticket exists and is
   `Done`. If there is none, create it as in the breakdown run and end the turn.
2. Post one project update with `linear_create_project_update`: what shipped, the
   sub-tickets that delivered it, and any follow-ups left open.
3. Move the parent to `Done`.

### Final verification run (title starts with `Final verification:`)

1. Do not open a PR. Check each requirement in the description against the default
   branch (code, tests, docs, behaviour) and record a requirement-by-requirement
   checklist with evidence in the workpad.
2. If anything is missing, file each gap with `linear_create_subissue`, list them in
   the workpad, and move this ticket to `Backlog`; a human re-promotes it once the
   gaps are closed.
3. When every requirement is met, move this ticket to `In Review` for a human to
   sign off and move it to `Done`. The parent's close-out run follows.
